TR1 is allocated to predominantly cystic and predominantly spongiform nodules as these are inherently benign, and no further points are assigned to them [ 9 ].
The ACR TI-RADS recommendations are as follows:
* TR1: no FNA;
* TR2: no FNA;
* TR3: if ≥1.5 cm, follow-up; if ≥2.5 cm, FNA; follow-up US at 1, 3, and 5 years;
* TR4: if ≥1.0 cm, follow-up; if ≥1.5 cm, FNA; follow-up US at 1, 2, 3, and 5 years;
* TR5: if ≥0.5 cm, follow-up; if ≥1.0 cm, FNA; US follow-up every year for the next 5 years.
An FNAB is required for suspicious nodules (TR3-TR5) that meet the size criteria mentioned above. Interval enlargement on follow-up is defined as meaningful if there is an increase of >20% in two dimensions and at least >2 mm in each dimension or if there is a >50% growth in volume [ 40 ]. If the ACR TI-RADS score increases in a follow-up US, an interval scan the following year is again required [ 40 ]. The scientific evidence for malignancy for each TR score is as follows: TR1: 0.3%; TR2: 1.5%; TR3: 4.8%; TR4: 9.1%; and TR5: 35% [ 41 ].
